  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You have 222 integers XXX and YYY.\n\nYou goal is to make them have a common divisor which is not 111, i.e. there should exist Z&gt;1Z &gt; 1Z&gt;1 such that ZZZ divides XXX and ZZZ divides YYY.\n\nIn one operation, you can do either of the following:\n\n\nSet XXX to X+1X + 1X+1.\nSet YYY to Y+1Y + 1Y+1.\nFind the minimum number of operations needed to achieve your goal.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains 222 integers - XXX and YYY.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the minimum number of operations.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1031 \\le T \\le 10^31≤T≤103",
      "1≤X,Y≤1091 \\le X, Y \\le 10^91≤X,Y≤109"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2 4\n1 1\n2 3",
        "output": "0\n2\n1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 1: The goal is already achieved since Z=2Z = 2Z=2 divides both.\nTest case 2: We first increment XXX and then YYY to get X=Y=2X = Y = 2X=Y=2 which has a common divisor Z=2Z = 2Z=2."
      }
    ]
  },
